# The cases the Compose pane has to EDIT, rather than merely draw. Each one is here because
# something in `M224` was wrong about it before the round, and the file is arranged so the four sit
# next to each other where a person clicking through will meet all of them.

# ── 1. A LOAD test with no workload at all ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `lenses.ts` is explicit that LOAD is carried by no statement: the lens comes from
# `test.workload !== null || test.thresholds.length > 0`, both properties of the declaration. So
# this test is on the LOAD door on the strength of its thresholds alone, and it is the one to open
# when you want to press `+ add to this test → workload` and watch bytes appear.
@catalogue @workload
test "a threshold-only test, waiting for a workload"
  api GET /products
  expect status equals 200
  threshold p95 duration is less than 2s
  threshold error rate is less than 5%

# ── 2. A test with neither clause — the bare target ─────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `+ add to this test → workload` lands here and writes the default `ramp to 5 users over 2s`. The
# pane does NOT refuse it, and that is worth knowing rather than assuming: `TF033` is a checker
# rule, so the file it leaves behind is one `tflw check` reports on. **The refusal is in the other
# direction**, and the test above carries it — see case 4.
@catalogue
test "a plain functional test, with neither clause"
  api GET /categories
  expect status equals 200

# ── 3. A workload test whose next neighbour is introduced by a comment ──────────────────────────
#
# This is the shape that broke `+ threshold` on 8 of 13 tests in the tflw repository's own example
# project, and it broke it everywhere, not only on LOAD. The anchor walked back over *whitespace*
# from the declaration's end, so on a test followed by a comment block belonging to the NEXT test
# it landed below that comment — inside the next declaration — and the splice came back
# `TF010 at line …`. `lastLineOfDecl` now walks back over the comment too, but only when a blank
# line separates it from this test's own last statement, which is what tells the two apart.
#
# Keep the comment block below this test where it is. It is the fixture.
@health @workload
test "the test above a comment block"
  hold 3 users for 1s
  api GET /health
  expect status equals 200
  threshold error rate is less than 5%

# This comment introduces the test below it, not the one above it — that is the whole distinction,
# and a blank line above it is how the splice knows. Editing either neighbour must leave it here,
# attached to the same test, in the same place.
@health @workload
test "the test below a comment block"
  hold 3 users for 1s
  api GET /health
  expect status equals 200
  threshold error rate is less than 5%

# ── 4. A scoped threshold, a note of its own, and where `TF033` actually refuses ────────────────
#
# A `p95 duration` threshold reads only the iterations that SUCCEEDED, so it cannot observe failure
# on its own and `TF033` requires an unscoped `error rate` one beside it. Scoping the duration to a
# tagged request narrows what it measures without narrowing what judges failure — here the catalogue
# read is timed and the second request beside it is not, so a slow neighbour cannot quietly pass as
# a slow catalogue.
#
# **This is the test to try `✕ thresholds` on.** The menu refuses, and the sentence points at the
# row that has to go first:
#
#     TF033 — a test that carries a `workload` must carry a threshold, so this one cannot be the
#     last thing removed. Take the workload off first — the row above.
#
# Take the workload off and press it again: the refusal becomes the ordinary *"empty it first — 2
# thresholds still here"*, because the rule that was speaking is no longer the one that applies.
# The check is *does this test carry a workload*, not *is this the last threshold* — a
# workload-bearing test with no thresholds is the state the rule forbids however it is arrived at.
@catalogue @workload
test "only the catalogue read is timed"
  ramp to 4 users over 2s
  api GET /products as "catalogue-read"
  expect status equals 200
  api GET /health
  expect status equals 200
  threshold p95 duration for "catalogue-read" is less than 2s
  threshold error rate is less than 5%
  # A note that belongs to THIS test and sits at the end of its own body — the other direction of
  # the same walk. Nothing follows it in this file, so there is no next declaration to mistake it
  # for, and an edit to the test above must not push it into one.
